# Generated whole-tree fingerprints.
#
# `priv/semantic_build_projection.json` embeds a `source_closure_hash` over the
# entire semantic source and dependency closure, so every branch that touches
# any input rewrites the same lines. Two branches changing unrelated files still
# collide there, every time.
#
# The file is derived entirely from other tracked inputs, so a textual merge of
# those hashes is meaningless -- regenerating is the only correct resolution.
#
# The primary defence is therefore not to merge it at all: ordinary branches do
# not regenerate it, because `ptc.gen_semantic_revision --check` runs only in
# the release gate (`.github/workflows/release.yml`), never in `precommit`,
# `prepush`, or PR CI. A branch that never rewrites these hashes cannot collide
# on them. Run `mix regen` on main before tagging a release.
#
# This matters because the driver below cannot help everywhere. A merge driver's
# definition lives in local config and is uncommittable, so GitHub's server-side
# merge does not have it: it sees `merge=ptc-generated`, finds no such driver,
# and silently falls back to a plain 3-way text merge that conflicts on every
# hash line. No local configuration can fix a conflict reported on a pull
# request -- only not touching the file can.
#
# The `ptc-generated` driver still keeps one side verbatim so that a local merge
# or rebase does not stop on the residual cases (a release commit, or a branch
# predating this policy).
#
# Deliberately NOT applied to `.duplication-baseline.json`: regenerating that
# file blesses whatever duplication the merged tree contains, which is a review
# decision rather than a derivation. Resolve it by hand.
#
# Registered per clone by `scripts/install-hooks.sh`, since Git merge drivers
# live in local config and cannot be committed.
priv/semantic_build_projection.json merge=ptc-generated
